<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="biostats-act-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>🏫 Activity 2 - Experiment Design</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> This assignment is due at the beginning of class on <alert> Monday, September 14 </alert>. It is graded based on completeness according to the rubric available on Canvas. It is open notes and collaboration with your group members is encouraged. </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A medical researcher hypothesizes that GLP-1 medications, which are designed to stabilize Insulin-Like Growth Factors (ILGFs) in diabetic patients, could be used to assist nondiabetic patients with weight loss. They have found 100 patients willing to participate in the study. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                For each level of measurement (nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio), list one measurement (relevant to the study) that could be recorded from the patients. Justify your answers.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which observation strategy (cross-sectional, retrospective, or prospective) would be best-suited for this experiment? Why? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                For each sampling method we have discussed (random, systematic, stratified, and cluster), design a sampling scheme that could be used to assign the treatment group. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Between all sampling schemes you designed above, which do you think would produce the least bias in the results? Why?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
